{% extends 'core/dashboard_base.htmx.j2' %}
{% block title %}Trends · Overview · sbomify{% endblock %}
{% block dashboard_content %}
    <c-overview.header active="trends" />
    {% comment %}
    HTMX does not swap a response that is not 2xx, so a failed fetch left the
    skeleton on screen for good: a broken page and a slow one looked identical,
    with nothing said and no way to retry. The frame holds both states and the
    error handlers pick between them. Retrying re-fires the same request
    through the frame's own trigger, so there is one request definition and the
    skeleton returns while it runs.
    {% endcomment %}
    <c-layout.frame x-data="{ failed: false }" hx-get="{% url 'vulnerability_scanning:vulnerability_trends' %}?show_product_filter=true" hx-trigger="load, retry-trends" hx-swap="innerHTML" @htmx:before-request="failed = false" @htmx:response-error="failed = true" @htmx:send-error="failed = true" @htmx:timeout="failed = true">
    <c-feedback.loading-chart message="Loading vulnerability trends..." x-show="!failed" />
    <template x-if="failed">
        <c-feedback.alert variant="danger" title="Could not load vulnerability trends">
        <c-slot name="action">
        <c-buttons.secondary size="sm" @click="$dispatch('retry-trends')">Try again</c-buttons.secondary>
        </c-slot>
        The request did not complete. Reload the page if this keeps happening.
        </c-feedback.alert>
    </template>
    </c-layout.frame>
{% endblock %}
